{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And much cattle belonged to the children of Reuben and the children of Gad — mighty, very.</b> Had it not been necessary to say 'and much and mighty cattle belonged,' we would not have said so — hear from this that each word stands on its own: for the children of Reuben had cattle great in number, while the children of Gad had cattle mighty in strength, in the manner of what is written (Job 1:10): ״וּמִקְנֵהוּ פָּרַץ בָּאָרֶץ״ — for his goats would kill wolves, as is derived in Ta'anit (25a): let the goats of Duba come with their horns. So too Gad had cattle mighty very in strength and power, and therefore they chose to dwell on the frontier, and the fear of enemies was not before their eyes — lest the wolves take their cattle, for they trusted in their strength. And in this one may find a certain merit on their behalf in that they put the cattle before the little ones, in their saying: ״גִּדְרוֹת צֹאן נִבְנֶה לְמִקְנֵנוּ וְעָרִים לְטַפֵּנוּ״. For they said: adjacent to the border of the frontier we will build first sheepfolds for our cattle, and afterward, in the interior of the land, we will build cities for our little ones — and thus we will ensure that the little ones dwell securely, for if the enemies come they will come first to the sheepfolds, and in the meantime the little ones will be saved, while the cattle on the frontier are not afraid of the wolves. Nevertheless, Moses rebuked them and said: if so, you do not trust in the salvation of HaShem; rather, thus shall you do: ״בְּנוּ לָכֶם עָרִים לְטַפְּכֶם״ first, near the frontier, ״וּגְדֵרוֹת לְצֹנַאֲכֶם״ in the interior — and your brothers, the whole house of Israel, will see that you trust in the salvation of HaShem, and thereby you will give courage to the hearts of your brothers that they may all trust in the salvation of HaShem." "'And what proceeds from your mouth you shall do' — for at first they said ״וְעָרִים לְטַפֵּנוּ״, which implies cities of any sort; but afterward they retracted their words and said: ״וְיָשַׁב טַפֵּנוּ בְּעָרֵי הַמִּבְצָר״, which implies that they intended to exalt themselves through the strength of the cities and would thereby bring faintness into the hearts of the men. Therefore Moses rebuked them and said: ״בְּנוּ לָכֶם עָרִים לְטַפְּכֶם וְגוֹ׳ וְהַיּוֹצֵא מִפִּיכֶם תַּעֲשׂוּ״ — for you have already said ״וְעָרִים לְטַפֵּנוּ״.'" "And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Tanhuma Matot 7) expounded concerning them the verse: ״נַחֲלָה מְבֹהֶלֶת בָּרִאשׁוֹנָה וְאַחֲרִיתָהּ לֹא תְבֹרָךְ״ (Proverbs 20:21) — because they took their inheritance first, therefore its end was not blessed, for they went at the head of all the exiles, as it says (II Chronicles 5:26): ״וַיַּגְלֵם לָרְאוּבֵנִי וְלַגָּדִי״. And in order to settle the vav of ״וְאַחֲרִיתָהּ״, it appears that this is its meaning: for according to our Rabbis of blessed memory they put the cattle before the little ones, and concerning this he said: ״נַחֲלָה מְבֹהֶלֶת בָּרִאשׁוֹנָה״ — for they put forward their inheritance, that is, the cattle, to be mentioned first; therefore also ״אַחֲרִיתָם״, that is, the little ones who came after, ״לֹא תְבֹרָךְ״ — for both their bodies and also their inheritance, all of it vanished like smoke; the principal perished along with the subsidiary, all of them went into captivity before a pursuer."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {2 "The verse cited as II Chronicles 5:26 follows the chapter division found in standard Hebrew texts (corresponding to II Chronicles 5:26 in some divisions, II Chronicles 36 in others); the address is given exactly as the author wrote it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:56.982108979Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}